        "addressCheck": "NO — neither Glatter nor any web source places a Gur shtiebel/synagogue at Levontin 14. The Glatter index entry for Gur contains NO street and NO house number at all — only \"16 בתי תפילה בראשית תש\\\"ך (1960)\", the Chidushei HaRim yeshiva, and the \"אחינו חסידי גור\" gemach (ref אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 2331, תיק 30-1960-2). So Glatter affirmatively does NOT support Levontin 14, and cannot support the corrected address either — Fierberg 17 comes only from the Tel Aviv-Jaffa Religious Council (rabanut.co.il), which I confirmed lists \"חסידי גור\" at \"פיירברג 17, לב העיר\", nusach Ashkenaz, gabbai Shlomo Schwartz. Levontin 14 is independently confirmed as \"Beit Sterling,\" a 1926 Magidovitch eclectic residential/commercial building (golden-lotus.co.il), with no religious use. The map pin (32.06184, 34.77569) falls in the Levontin/Gan HaHashmal area, consistent with Beit Sterling but unrelated to Gur. The mapErrorFlag \"not-a-synagogue\" and confidence \"low\" are correct.",
        "sourceQuality": "High and honest. All 5 cited URLs resolve and genuinely support their claims: (1) rabanut.co.il confirms Gur at Fierberg 17, nusach Ashkenaz; (2) golden-lotus.co.il confirms Beit Sterling, Magidovitch, 1926, Tzvi Sterling, restored 2009 into 7 apartments + 2 shops, NO synagogue; (3) tlv-streets.yonbergman.com is about Z.D. Levontin the man, mentions no synagogue on Levontin (only the Great Synagogue on Allenby), consistent with the claim; (4)+(5) Hebrew Wikipedia on Chidushei HaRim yeshiva and Gur Hasidism corroborate the dynasty background. Independent search confirmed the yeshiva began in the Gur shtiebel on HaTavor St, moved to Peretz 12 in 1939 (building inaugurated 1953), and that Gur's TA institutions are on Bar-Ilan, Fierberg and Yavne — matching the research. No fabricated or dead URLs found.",
        "corrections": "The research is a correct DEBUNK and needs no factual correction — its conclusion (Levontin 14 is not a Gur institution; it is Beit Sterling) is right. One nuance to tighten: the \"correctedAddress\" should NOT be presented as Glatter-supported. Glatter gives no address for Gur at all; Fierberg 17 is sourced solely from the present-day Religious Council — a contemporary administrative listing, not Glatter, and not necessarily the 1940-1965 location. On the period framing: Imrei Emet led 1940-1948 (died Shavuot 5708/1948), Beit Yisrael succeeded 1948-1965 — the dynastyBackground states this correctly. Founder empty and foundingYear \"unknown\" are appropriate since no institution exists at this address. No anachronisms or conflations introduced by the research.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty attribution to גור (Gur/Ger) is historically accurate: founded 1859 by Rabbi Yitzchak Meir Alter (Chidushei HaRim) in Góra Kalwaria, Poland; led 1940-1948 by Avraham Mordechai Alter (Imrei Emet), then Israel Alter (Beit Yisrael) from 1948. Rebbe sat in Jerusalem; Tel Aviv a secondary center. Confirmed via Hebrew Wikipedia. BUT the dynasty is NOT tied to Levontin 14 — the attribution is only to the dynasty's general TA presence, not this address.",
        "founderCheck": "No founder is claimed (founder field empty) — correct, because no Gur institution is verified at Levontin 14. The dynasty founder (Yitzchak Meir Alter, Chidushei HaRim, 1859) is real and correctly described in the background, and is NOT falsely attached to this address. No fabricated founder.",
